<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">5 February 1794 · National Convention, Paris</p><h1 class="sp-title">Robespierre on Virtue and Terror</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Maximilien Robespierre</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>With France at war with much of Europe and civil war raging in the west, the Committee of Public Safety governed by emergency decree and the guillotine. Speaking for the committee, Maximilien Robespierre set out to the National Convention the principles that he said must guide the Republic in a time of revolution.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>If the mainspring of popular government in peacetime is virtue, the mainspring of popular government in revolution is at once virtue and terror: virtue, without which terror is fatal; terror, without which virtue is powerless.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Report on the Principles of Political Morality, 17 Pluviôse Year II (5 February 1794).</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Speeches_of_Maximilien_Robespierre/Report_on_the_Principles_of_Political_Morality" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The Terror reached its height that summer, after a law of June 1794 stripped defendants before the Revolutionary Tribunal of almost every protection. On 9 Thermidor (27 July 1794) the Convention turned on Robespierre, and he was guillotined the next day; the Terror was dismantled in the months that followed.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thermidorian-reaction">Thermidorian Reaction</a></div></div></section>
